<com:TContent ID="body" >

<h1>TExpression</h1>
<com:DocLink ClassPath="Prado.Web.UI.WebControls.TExpression" />

<p class="block-content">
<tt>TExpression</tt> evaluates a PHP expression and displays the evaluation result. To specify the expression to be evaluated, set the <tt>Expression</tt> property. Note, <tt>TExpression</tt> evaluates the expression during the rendering control lifecycle.
</p>

<p class="block-content">
The context of the expression in a <tt>TExpression</tt> control is the control itself. That is, <tt>$this</tt> represents the control object if it is present in the expression. For example, the following template tag will display the title of the page containing the <tt>TExpression</tt> control.
</p>
<com:TTextHighlighter Language="prado" CssClass="source block-content">
&lt;com:TExpression Expression="$this->Page->Title" /&gt;
</com:TTextHighlighter>

<p class="block-content">
Be aware, since <tt>TExpression</tt> allows execution of arbitrary PHP code, in general you should not use it to evaluate expressions submitted by your application users.
</p>

<h2>Choosing between the expression forms</h2>

<p class="block-content">
A template has three ways to put the result of PHP into a page. They differ in when they
run and in what they can reach.
</p>

<table class="parameters">
<tr><th>Form</th><th>Runs</th><th>Notes</th></tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>&lt;%= $expr %&gt;</tt></td>
	<td>On every render</td>
	<td>The shortest form. <tt>$this</tt> is the template control rather than a separate control object.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>&lt;%# $expr %&gt;</tt></td>
	<td>Once, when the control is data bound</td>
	<td>A databind expression. The result is kept in view state. It sets a control property rather than standing in free text.</td>
</tr>
<tr>
	<td><tt>&lt;com:TExpression&gt;</tt></td>
	<td>On every render</td>
	<td>A control, so it takes an id, holds view state, and is shown or hidden through <tt>Visible</tt>.</td>
</tr>
</table>

<p class="block-content">
<a href="?page=Controls.Statements">TStatements</a> is the matching control for several
statements. It captures whatever the statements print rather than evaluating a single
expression.
</p>

